# Area rules (read before work)

Lasting project rules live only under `technical-documentation/**` as `documentation-rules.md` and `technical-rules.md`. This file does **not** restate them.

## Before planning or any implementation

1. Resolve the **working area** from the task (paths you will touch, or the docs folder in scope).
2. **Read** the nearest `documentation-rules.md` and `technical-rules.md` for that area (see resolution below). Use the Read tool — do not skim from memory.
3. In your **first reply** for the task (or before the plan), state briefly that you have read those files and will follow them. Name the paths.
4. Only then plan or edit. If the area shifts mid-task, re-resolve and re-ack for the new pair.

## Resolve which files

**Precedence:** nearest section file **supersedes** parents on overlap; root masters apply where the section is silent. Nested sections win the same way.

| If work is under… | Start from |
|-------------------|------------|
| `technical-documentation/<section>/…` | That section’s pair; walk **up** toward `technical-documentation/` until each of the two files exists |
| `frontend/` | `technical-documentation/frontend/` |
| `services/` | `technical-documentation/backend/` |
| `deployment-tool/`, `scripts/deployment-tool/` | `technical-documentation/deployment/deployment-tool/` |
| `.github/` | `technical-documentation/deployment/github-actions/` |
| `docker-stack*.yml`, `docker-bake.hcl`, compose at repo root | `technical-documentation/stack/` |
| Unclear / cross-cutting | Root `technical-documentation/documentation-rules.md` + `technical-documentation/technical-rules.md`; add every area pair you will actually edit |

Always open both files for the resolved area (docs discipline + engineering). If only one exists at a level, use that level’s file and keep walking up for the missing one.

Cross-area tasks: read **each** relevant nearest pair (still ack once, listing all paths).
